WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:02.489
This is a small juice plant.

2
00:00:03.309 --> 00:00:06.432
On the left stands a mixing vat, on the right a filler,

3
00:00:06.641 --> 00:00:09.706
and in between the juice flows through a row of equipment.

4
00:00:10.774 --> 00:00:13.572
Each step in that row is a unit operation:

5
00:00:13.757 --> 00:00:17.589
a basic physical step that comes back in every process plant.

6
00:00:18.357 --> 00:00:21.631
Here they are mixing, pumping, heating, holding and cooling.

7
00:00:22.664 --> 00:00:25.079
Separating is a unit operation too.

8
00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:28.822
It has many forms: filtering through a cloth or sieve,

9
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:33.524
centrifuging by spinning fast, distilling by boiling and catching the vapour,

10
00:00:33.629 --> 00:00:37.402
or evaporating, where you boil off water to concentrate a liquid.

11
00:00:38.457 --> 00:00:40.117
Other plants dry their product,

12
00:00:40.326 --> 00:00:44.192
or let substances react: those are unit operations too.

13
00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:49.466
A dairy, a chemical plant and a water works use the same building blocks,

14
00:00:49.571 --> 00:00:50.941
just in a different order.

15
00:00:52.281 --> 00:00:54.115
Look at the mixing vat first.

16
00:00:54.860 --> 00:01:00.213
It works as a batch process: a fixed amount goes in, is processed step by step,

17
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.291
and comes out again as one lot.

18
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:05.736
Fill it, mix it, then empty the vat.

19
00:01:06.516 --> 00:01:08.629
That whole lot gets one batch number,

20
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.346
with one record of what went in and what happened.

21
00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:15.018
That is flexible and easy to trace.

22
00:01:15.766 --> 00:01:19.354
It suits many recipes and small volumes: in pharma,

23
00:01:19.505 --> 00:01:22.605
specialty chemicals, and much of the food industry.

24
00:01:23.589 --> 00:01:27.269
The downside: filling, emptying and cleaning produce nothing,

25
00:01:27.432 --> 00:01:29.916
and every batch comes out a little different.

26
00:01:31.271 --> 00:01:34.092
From the pump on it is a continuous process.

27
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:38.672
The juice flows in and out all the time: through the heat exchanger,

28
00:01:38.905 --> 00:01:41.272
the holding tube and the cooler, to the filler.

29
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:45.336
Once the line runs, it sits at steady state.

30
00:01:46.086 --> 00:01:51.323
The temperatures, pressures and flows hold steady at every point, hour after hour,

31
00:01:51.485 --> 00:01:52.774
and nothing builds up.

32
00:01:53.826 --> 00:01:59.179
That is efficient at large volumes: in refineries, paper mills, bulk chemicals,

33
00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:00.282
and milk powder.

34
00:02:01.265 --> 00:02:03.761
But starting up and shutting down take hours,

35
00:02:03.947 --> 00:02:07.244
and what you make in that time does not meet the specification.

36
00:02:07.977 --> 00:02:12.180
That is called off-spec: it must be reworked, or sold off cheaply.

37
00:02:12.871 --> 00:02:14.624
So such lines hate stopping.

38
00:02:15.698 --> 00:02:17.323
Many plants combine the two,

39
00:02:17.462 --> 00:02:21.688
as here: a mixing vat that works in batches feeds a continuous line.

40
00:02:22.993 --> 00:02:25.234
How long does the product spend in a step?

41
00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:31.204
Suppose this vat runs continuously: as much flows in as flows out, all the time.

42
00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:36.070
That time is the residence time: on average the volume of the step,

43
00:02:36.268 --> 00:02:39.682
divided by the flow, the volume that passes through per hour.

44
00:02:40.423 --> 00:02:45.497
A tank of 6 cubic metres, with 3 cubic metres per hour flowing through,

45
00:02:45.741 --> 00:02:46.844
gives 2 hours.

46
00:02:47.853 --> 00:02:50.024
More flow means less residence time.

47
00:02:50.696 --> 00:02:53.169
Push 50 percent more through the same tank,

48
00:02:53.343 --> 00:02:58.602
and every litre spends a third less time inside: here one hour and twenty minutes.

49
00:02:59.616 --> 00:03:04.179
So ask about the residence time whenever quality depends on time: in heating,

50
00:03:04.307 --> 00:03:06.374
keeping hot, reacting or settling.

51
00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:10.385
But watch out: residence time is an average.

52
00:03:11.128 --> 00:03:13.404
In a stirred tank some of the product shoots

53
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.144
from the inlet to the outlet in a few minutes.

54
00:03:17.149 --> 00:03:22.350
Another part hangs for hours in the dead corners, where the agitator hardly reaches.

55
00:03:23.398 --> 00:03:27.577
If product must stay somewhere for a minimum time, as in pasteurising,

56
00:03:27.682 --> 00:03:32.511
heating to kill germs, you design on the fastest part, not on the average.

57
00:03:33.836 --> 00:03:35.229
Back to the juice plant.

58
00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:40.464
After heating, the juice must stay hot for a set time, to kill germs.

59
00:03:41.217 --> 00:03:46.116
So it flows through the holding tube: 0.1 cubic metres inside,

60
00:03:46.279 --> 00:03:49.216
with 12 cubic metres per hour flowing through.

61
00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:53.499
The residence time is the volume divided by the flow.

62
00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:59.350
That gives 30 seconds, and the food safety plan demands at least 20.

63
00:04:00.346 --> 00:04:03.411
If production wants 18 cubic metres per hour,

64
00:04:03.597 --> 00:04:08.880
the residence time becomes 20 seconds: exactly on the minimum, with no margin.

65
00:04:09.894 --> 00:04:11.659
And that is still an average.

66
00:04:12.399 --> 00:04:15.093
In the centre of the tube the juice flows faster,

67
00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:19.006
so the fastest juice spends less than 20 seconds inside.

68
00:04:19.768 --> 00:04:23.506
That is why you design a holding tube on the fastest particle.

69
00:04:24.548 --> 00:04:28.123
In a continuous process, throughput, the output per hour,

70
00:04:28.309 --> 00:04:30.376
and residence time are tied together.

71
00:04:31.112 --> 00:04:35.281
More output needs a longer holding tube, not just a faster pump.

72
00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:38.984
Experts do not always agree either.

73
00:04:39.741 --> 00:04:44.083
In pharma the debate is whether plants should move from batch to continuous.

74
00:04:45.101 --> 00:04:49.919
Regulators such as the US FDA encourage continuous manufacturing.

75
00:04:50.596 --> 00:04:54.358
But auditors know the batch record best: one lot, one file.

76
00:04:55.358 --> 00:04:58.934
So a sharp question in any plant is: which steps here are batch,

77
00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.558
which are continuous, and where do they meet?

78
00:05:02.605 --> 00:05:07.586
In this juice plant the mixing vat is batch, and the rest of the line is continuous.

79
00:05:08.286 --> 00:05:10.445
They meet at the pump under the vat.
